\chapter{Conclusion}
  \label{chap:conclusion}
  % The three research questions here can be answered now
    % Subject pronouns mark one as Creole or Cajun
    % Personal social networks are ethnically homophilous, but the amount of homophily is not predictive of subject pronoun use, likely due to the lack of a continuous gradient in homophily
      % In other words, personal networks are essentially all highly ethnically homophilous
    % The discourse of Creoles and Cajuns is indicative of racialization, especially in how they mark boundaries
    % The overal picture from these answers suggests that Creoles and Cajuns are racialized, but more work can be done to further establish this as more than suggestive or to build off of what has been found here

  \section{Subject pronouns}
    % The sample of participants is not a true random sample that can produce generalizable results
      % This is the case for most variationist studies, so it does not invalidate the findings but rather highlights the need for replication studies
    % As the first time that a robust variationist analysis of F\&C has been carried out, this study presents unique data on how subject pronouns are distributed between groups in areas where both French and Creole are said to be spoken
      % The broad US categories of Black and White may be even more predictive of pronoun usage than Creole and Cajun, the latter categories are still predictive as would be expected when these groups are racialized
      % Despite the consistent evidence for the racialization of Creoles and Cajuns, more can be done to understand the role that broad US racial categories play in this racialization
        % There was a clear association of Cajuns with White and Creoles with non-White, but the US categories provided by participants were simplified due to the size of the sample of participants
          % As Giancarlo did for Creoles of color, it would likely be fruitful to examine a large same of Creoles defined in any way and even of Cajuns
            % Cajuns were more straightforwardly White in their self-identifications, but Cajun itself was also sometimes part of if not the whole of their racial self-identifications
            % Likewise, not all Creoles were "of color", in broad US terms
    % Much like Rottet found in Terrebonne-Lafourche, location likely has predictive value for subject pronouns
      % This could not be detected here because the sample of participants was not large enough to have a sufficient count of both Creoles and Cajuns in each of several towns
        % Such an analysis would likely have to use town as a delimiter rather than parish as, anecdotally, there appeared to be differences even between neighboring towns, e.g. Arnaudville seemed more likely to have speakers with Cajun-marked pronouns and Cecilia was repeatedly described as having speakers of Creole
    % Using a much narrower geographical range for sampling, i.e. examining a single town, would be a more practical approach short of having significant funding to collect large quantities of data from several towns each
      % Specific towns such as St Martinville or Breaux Bridge would be ideal
        % St Martinville is the home of the Acadian Memorial and the Evangeline Oak juxtaposed against an African-American museum that gives a central place to Creoles in its exhibits
          % St Martinville plays up the presence of F\&C, as well, with significant French (but not Creole) signage throughout the central area of the town
        % Breaux Bridge was the site of one of the most important documentations of Creole, and several(?) participants were associated with this town
          % This included Errol, who was a White Cajun but used Creole-marked pronouns
          % Likewise, interactions with locals who did not become participants suggest that Errol's linguistic behavior relative to his identity was not unusual
        % Other potentially fruitful towns would be Henderson or Cecilia
          % The structure of Cecilia, with a lack of obvious public gathering locations, may make it difficult to find participants
          % Henderson is more open in this regard, with the popular restaurant Pat's and the adjoining and equally popular large dance hall the Atchafalaya Club, but my own visits to the town and interactions with the people suggest that the people there are particularly wary of strangers asking about their language
            % Also unlike the other three towns, the proportion of the population of Henderson that identified as Black or African-American on the census is not nearly as high, meaning that while there may be many who use Creole-marked speech, there may be few who identify as Creole given its modern connection to non-White identity
    % This should include a larger range of ages
      % Rottet was able to use a middle age range (30-54) as a bin, and while Dajko chose to focus on speakers over 60 due in part to difficulties in finding younger Cajun speakers, she did report finding Indians in their 30s who only learned English once they went began school (65)
        % However, the present study had a noticeable gap in the middle age ranges despite making no attempts to prioritize older individuals, possibly for several reasons
          % 1) There may be a lack of speakers in that age range due to how valuation of the F\&C has shifted back and forth over time
            % A very common personal story for those under 40 or even 50 is recalling how their grandparents spoke F\&C but no one else, a story that is even applicable to myself
            % The younger people that I did find who spoke F\%C were either Black, products of immersion schooling, or particularly motivated individuals who had taken it upon themselves to teach themselves the language
              % For these last two categories, Gudmestad & Carmichael (2022), in agreement with Kasstan (2019), said that we should look at the speech of speakers who have learned French through revitalization programs (21)
                % That is also the view taken by myself
          % 2) Those in the middle age ranges are likely to be busier than older folks
            % This means they may have less time available, be less willing to share what time they do have available, and/or not be spending as much time in public locations or at public events where they might be recruited for participation
  \section{Social networks}
    % As already noted, there is a dearth of social network data for Louisiana, especially social network data that codes for local social categories
      % Part of the aim of this study was to begin the process of filling in this gap in information
      % The network data presented here gives a consistent image of Creoles primarily associating with other Creoles and almost never with Cajuns and of Cajuns primarily associated with other Cajuns and only rarely taking on Creoles as acquaintences
        % This is valuable information, but not yet generalizable
    % Social network data should continue to be collected
      % However, social network data collection is difficult to get right, and in the case of collecting whole network data, is very time consuming
        % There are indications here, though, that speakers of F\&C, especially Cajuns, have relatively short social distances between them
        % The continued collection of this data can provide a much better picture of those distances
          % This data would naturally have value for future research, which could focus in on social structure by analyzing various forms of segregation in relation to social networks
            % For instance, homophily in personal networks is unsurprising with racialized groups, but it can occur for a number of other reasons, whereas segregation is perhaps more related to the sort of conflict that underlies racialization
          % Beyond research, though, this data may be informative for how to approach F\&C revitalization efforts
            % It is common for F\&C speakers to report having no one with whom to speak the language, but even the very fact that participants can be successfully recruited for studies such as this one is evidence that there are often F\&C speakers hiding in plain sight
              % Knowing more about this can provide avenues to target when attempting to put F\&C speakers in greater contact with each other
            % Network data is also effective for uncovering where languages are being passed along, whether to children, grandchildren, or to any other connection
              % Such knowledge is essential for building effective revitalization programs, especially when resources for such programs are limited and so cannot be wasted on ineffective initiatives
  \section{Discourse}
    % The discourse data presented here was particularly rich and was again consistent with the racialization of Creoles and Cajuns
    % In terms of boundary marking, boundary policing, and boundary maintenance, most of what was uncovered here was marking due to the nature of the data collection procedure generally involving discourse with the researcher only
      % Cajuns were treated as a default, White/European group whose Whiteness/Europeanness did not need to be explicitly laid out, whereas Creoles were more explicitly characterized in essentialist terms of blood and color
    % What could still be added to this picture is discourse showing how these boundaries are policed and maintained
      % Essentially, group interviews are needed